    "abstract": "<jats:p>Migraine is a prevalent neurological disorder characterized by recurrent and disabling attacks of moderate to severe headache. Migraine attacks are triggerable, a feature systematically investigated in human models of migraine. This has led to the discovery of several endogenous and exogenous triggers of migraine and the development of treatments blocking the migraine-inducing substance calcitonin gene-related peptide (CGRP). Yet despite the success of treatments targeting CGRP signaling, many patients still experience inadequate or no response. Most recently, the model has highlighted another promising target: pituitary adenylate cyclase activating polypeptide (PACAP). Results from a recent phase II trial show that blocking PACAP can reduce the frequency of migraine attacks, suggesting that anti-PACAP medication might soon join the armamentarium of migraine treatments. Given that CGRP and PACAP act through partly different signaling mechanisms, this holds great potential for expanding treatment options for individuals with migraine.</jats:p>",
